ENCAPSULATION-
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HOkFfun4bjo
Encapsulation means keeping an object's data protected and controlling how that data can be accessed or changed.
"PROBLEM"-
suppose we have a BankAccount-
class BankAccount {
    double balance;
}

now someone can do-
BankAccount account = new BankAccount();
account.balance = -50000;

A bank account shouldn't normally allow someone to directly change its balance to anything they want.
We want the class to say:
"You can't directly touch my balance. If you want to change it, you have to go through my rules." 
That's where encapsulation comes in.

"ENCAPSULATION = DATA HIDING + CONTROLLED ACCESS"
instead of-
class BankAccount {
    double balance;
}
we make balance private-
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;
}
Now outside code cannot directly access it-
BankAccount account = new BankAccount();
account.balance = 5000;  // ❌ Error ( because of "private double balance;" )
"balance can only be directly accessed from inside the BankAccount class." 
This is called data hiding.

"HOW DO WE ACCESS THE DATA?" 
If balance is private, how can we read or modify it?
" We provide methods that control access."
class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance = balance + amount;
        }
    }
}
now-
BankAccount account = new BankAccount();
account.deposit(5000);
System.out.println(account.getBalance());
Output- 5000.0
but this is not allowed-
account.balance = -50000;  // ❌
=> the class controlls how balance can change!

"KEY IDEA"
Think of your class as a bank vault.
              BankAccount
        +-----------------------+
        |                       |
        |  private balance      |
        |                       |
        |  🔒 5000              |
        |                       |
        +-----------------------+
             ↑             ↑
             |             |
        getBalance()   deposit()
You don't allow someone to walk into the vault and change the money directly.
Instead, they interact through controlled operations:
account.deposit(1000); 
     OR
account.getBalance(); 
"That's the encapsulation"

"🔵GETTERS"
=> A getter is a method used to retrieve/read a private field.
Example-
class Student {

    private String name;
    private int age;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}
now-
Student s = new Student();
System.out.println(s.getName());
System.out.println(s.getAge());
-> so this gives controlled access to the data.

"🔵SETTERS"
=> A setter is a method used to modify/change a private field.
Example-
class Student {

    private String name;
    private int age;

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }
}
then-
Student s = new Student();
s.setName("Raga");
s.setAge(24);

we are not doing-
s.name = "Raga"; 

we are doing-
s.setName("Rahul");
-> The class gets a chance to control what happens.

"THE REAL POWER OF SETTERS" 
=> suppose we have-
class Student {

    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }
}
There's nothing stopping someone from doing:
s.setAge(-500);
That's still bad data.
So we can put validation inside the setter.
class Student {

    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {

        if (age >= 0) {
            this.age = age;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Age cannot be negative");
        }
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}
Now:-
s.setAge(20);    // ✅
s.setAge(-500);  // ❌ rejected
=> This is the real benefit of encapsulation
=> The class controls its own data.

"ENCAPSULATION IS NOT "SIMPLE PRIVATE VARIABLES""
This is an important distinction.
you'll sometimes hear- Encapsulation means making variables private 
Thats the part of it, but not the complete idea!
Better Definition-
Encapsulaton is bundling data and the methods that operate on that data inside a class, while restricting direct access to the internal state and providing controlled access where appropriate.
class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        // rules
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        // rules
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
Here:
- balance → data
- deposit() → behavior
- withdraw() → behavior
- getBalance() → controlled access
Everything related to the bank account is bundled into the BankAccount class.
That's encapsulation.

"ENCAPSULATION DOESN'T MEAN EVERY FIELD NEEDS A SETTER"
for example-
private String name;

public String getName() {
    return name;
}

public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}
people think that- every private variable needs getter + setter 
no ❌
You should expose only what makes sense.


















